ing  property specific estimators so for a  particular property a very fine-tuned  estimator for that property which  achieves optimal and  efficient time complexity was studied  so  what  we study is  given some class of properties  can we design an efficient  sample optimal universal estimator for  those  class of properties okay  so in this talk i will mainly focus on  discrete distributions and i'll assume  that my samples are iid and the class of  properties will be estimating  our so-called symmetric properties that  i'll define in a minute  okay so  let me formally define this question so  you have some hidden discrete  distribution p  defined over a domain d  and you are given some n iid symbols  from this distribution which i denote by  xn  and i'm going to refer to it as a  sequence from here on and the goal is to  estimate some f of p where f is a  function and i'm going to refer to it as  a property of the distribution okay and  as i mentioned earlier we are only  interested in properties called as  symmetric properties these are  properties that are invariant to label  permutations which do not depend on the  label information  so here are two  popular symmetric properties so one is  the support which is the number of  non-zero probability values in a  distribution and also entropy and note  that if you permute the labels the  property value doesn't change  so  [Music]  for these properties and many other  well-known symmetric properties which  have written their  time and sample complexity for  estimating them is already studied well  understood and we have property specific  optimal sample optimal estimators  but the question we are interested in is  can you come up with one estimator that  works  for all these properties simultaneous  properties simultaneously and achieves  optimal sample complexity okay  now  what do i mean by one estimator  so  given a samples  you want to output a distribution p hat  independent of what property you want to  estimate  and the hope that when you output the  property value f of p hat it's close to  the property value of the hidden  distribution so this is what i mean by  universal estimation so you output a  distribution independent of the property  and hope that  when you calculate the property value on  this output and distribution it's close  to the hidden distribution  okay so let me uh switch gears and  define a different maximum likelihood  than what you have traditionally seen  i'll tell you  it's interesting in its own right and  later i'll tell you how it's related to  this  task of symmetric property estimation  okay so let me define this  through an example  consider a domain  d with three symbols  which is  abc  suppose you receive some three id  symbols from this distribution let this  samples be aab which i'm going to refer  to as a sequence  the profile  of a sequence  is just the multi set of frequencies so  in the sample aab  you see that a appears twice and b  appears once so the profile of that  sequence is just 2 comma 1 okay  now  what is the probability of observing  sequence sequence aab  since these samples are iid it's just p  a square pv  and now you can ask what is the  probability of observing profile two  comma one uh it's just uh  consider all the sequences that will  give me two comma one as the profile and  add up their probability values okay  so i have written down  the sequences which will give me the  profile 2 comma 1. for instance you will  note aac also gives me the probability  profile 2 comma 1 so i'll basically add  the probability corresponding to all  these sequences that's my probability of  a profile  okay  now  you have already seen sequence maximum  likelihood  which is basically tries to find a  distribution that maximizes the  likelihood of observing xn the sequence  xn the profile maximum likelihood  uh it's just the distribution that  maximizes the likelihood of observing  the profile fee okay  now what's interesting is that 